# Git hooks must keep LF endings.
#
# This repository is developed on Windows with `core.autocrlf=true`, which rewrites
# checked-out text files to CRLF. A shell script with CRLF endings fails at its shebang
# line — `/bin/sh^M: bad interpreter` — so `.githooks/pre-push` would silently stop
# refusing non-main pushes on the next fresh clone, which is the exact moment the rule
# matters most.
#
# Scoped to `.githooks/` on purpose: a repository-wide eol setting would rewrite every
# tracked text file in one commit.
.githooks/** text eol=lf

# The portable secrets backup must survive the trip byte for byte.
#
# The goal is that downloading this repository as a ZIP onto a clean machine and running
# `secrets:restore` works with only the passphrase. `core.autocrlf=true` here, and the
# recovery key is PEM — plain text as far as Git is concerned — so without `-text` it is
# checked out with CRLF endings that never existed in the blob. Node's PEM parser happens
# to tolerate that, which is worse than if it did not: the corruption would be invisible
# until a restore on a machine that mattered.
#
# `-text` disables end-of-line conversion in both directions, for checkout and for the
# `git archive` export GitHub's "Download ZIP" serves.
config/secret-archive-latest.zip.enc -text
config/secret-archive-latest.zip.enc.private-key.pem -text

# The public key is listed for the same reason, though it is not on the restore path.
# It was checked out CRLF here (814 bytes) while the blob and every ZIP download carry LF
# (800), and `keyId` in the archive header is a sha256 over those exact bytes — so the
# committed key hashed to something no other machine could reproduce. Nothing broke,
# because decryption never reads the public key or checks `keyId`, but a fingerprint that
# depends on where it was read from is not a fingerprint.
config/secret-archive-public.pem -text
